.TH HEADROOM 1 "2026-10-25" "Headroom 0.1.1-beta.5" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
headroom \- weekly Claude and Codex allowance left, against the time left until renewal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B headroom
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-refresh ]
.br
.B headroom statusline
.RB [ \-\-format
.IR claude | tmux | sketchybar | plain ]
.RB [ \-\-provider
.IR claude | codex ]
.RB [ \-\-run
.IR id ]
.br
.B headroom wait
.RB [ \-\-provider
.IR claude | codex | any ]
.RB [ \-\-timeout
.IR duration ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B headroom wait \-\-run
.I id
.RB [ \-\-until
.IR done | attention ]
.RB [ \-\-timeout
.IR duration ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B headroom tasks
.RB [ \-\-run
.IR id ]
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-watch ]
.br
.B headroom task
.IR plan | add | start | progress | wait | done | fail | skip | cancel
.RI [ id ...]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B headroom task
.IR status | guide | adopt | hook
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B headroom hooks
.IR install | uninstall | status
.RB [ \-\-codex ]
.RB [ \-\-enforce ]
.RB [ \-\-instructions ]
.RB [ \-\-settings
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-project ]
.br
.B headroom mcp
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B headroom
prints what Headroom.app knows about your Claude and OpenAI Codex plans: how much of
the weekly allowance is left, how long until it renews, the pacing verdict, the 5-hour
session, model caps and reset credits.
.PP
It reads only the snapshot the app writes to
.IR "~/Library/Application Support/Headroom/snapshot.json" .
It never reads credentials and never uses the network. When the app isn't running it
prints the last snapshot and says how old it is.
.PP
It also tracks long agent runs: an agent declares a tree of tasks with
.BR "headroom task" ,
hooks and the app record what the harness and its subagents do, and
.B headroom tasks
shows each run's state, progress and forecast. Each report an agent makes prints one line
back (at most three): what changed, the run's percent, what is still open, a task that has
gone 20 minutes without a report, and the room left in the 5-hour window when the app knows
it.
.B \-\-quiet
prints nothing (for
.BR add ,
just the tag). Agent text is stripped of control characters and capped: titles at 120
characters, notes, evidence and reasons at 400. Flags take
.B \-\-flag value
or
.BR \-\-flag=value ,
and
.B "headroom task \fIaction\fP \-\-help"
explains one action.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B headroom
One line per provider (emoji cells, percent left, time to renewal), then indented lines
for the verdict, the 5-hour session, blocks, faults and reset credits. The session line
is a warning from the level set in Settings (75% used by default), an amber heads-up when
the recent pace runs it out before its reset, and a dim line with the spare share otherwise.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Every number in the snapshot, plus
.B stale
and
.BR age_seconds .
Schema version 1. Each session carries
.BR outlook " (idle, comfortable, lasts, runs_out, blocked),"
.B runs_out_at
and
.BR left_at_reset .
.TP
.B \-\-refresh
Asks the app for fresh limits (a Darwin notification,
.BR com.scalexy.headroom.refresh ),
then waits up to 10 seconds for a new snapshot. Requests closer than 30 seconds apart
reuse the current snapshot.
.TP
.B statusline
One short line for a prompt, tmux, SketchyBar or Claude Code's status line. It reads and
ignores Claude Code's JSON on standard input. Colors use ANSI (claude) or tmux style
codes (tmux); sketchybar and plain have none.
.B NO_COLOR
turns colors off. When a task run is active for the run id
.RB ( \-\-run ,
Claude Code's
.B session_id
on standard input, or the variables under
.BR ENVIRONMENT ),
it appends a segment: percent done, running tasks, and
.B ⏸input
when the run needs you.
.TP
.B wait
Blocks while the 5-hour session or the weekly limit is at 100%, or a major outage is
active, then returns. It wakes when the snapshot changes or at the reset time and uses
no CPU while waiting. With
.B \-\-provider any
(the default) it returns as soon as either provider has capacity.
.B \-\-json
prints
.B {"blocked_by", "until"}
first.
.TP
.B wait \-\-run \fIid\fP
Blocks until the run is finished (done, partial or failed). With
.B \-\-until attention
it also returns when the run needs input or the agent stopped with work left. Exits 2
on timeout. Without
.B \-\-run
it waits for capacity as described above.
.TP
.B tasks
Active runs as trees: a state glyph, the title, a bar of work done, in flight and not
started, the percent done (and the token-based estimate when it is ahead), must-haves
(critical tasks done), the forecast, and tokens. Each task shows its steps, waits, failures
and tokens.
.B \-\-all
shows every run from the last 7 days,
.B \-\-json
prints the folded runs (the
.I tasks.json
schema), and
.B \-\-watch
redraws when anything changes. Runs come from
.I tasks.json
while Headroom.app runs; otherwise the CLI folds the event files itself and says so.
.TP
.B task plan
Declares many tasks from a JSON tree on standard input or
.B \-\-file
and prints each task's tag,
.BI [hr: run : path ] ,
for the agent spawned for it. Child ids are relative to their parent and must be unique;
unknown keys are reported and ignored. It refuses to wait on a terminal or on a pipe
nobody writes to. Task keys are
.BR id ,
.BR title ,
.BR effort ,
.BR importance ,
.B steps
and
.BR children :
.RS
.nf
{"title": "Refactor auth", "tasks": [{"id": "api", "title": "API",
  "effort": "l", "importance": "critical", "steps": 3,
  "children": [{"id": "tests", "title": "API tests"}]}]}
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B task add \fIid\fP \-\-title \fItext\fP
Declares a task, or updates one (a known task needs no title).
.B \-\-effort
is xs, s, m, l, xl (1, 2, 4, 8, 16) or a number, compared only among siblings;
.B \-\-importance
is critical, normal or optional;
.B \-\-steps
is the number of steps. Ids are paths:
.I api/tests
is a child of
.IR api .
.TP
.B task start|done|fail|skip|cancel \fIid\fP ...
Marks one or more tasks.
.B done
takes
.BR \-\-evidence ,
.B fail
needs
.BR \-\-reason ,
.B skip
and
.B cancel
take one. A task with open tasks under it is refused unless
.BR \-\-force ,
which closes them the same way.
.TP
.B task progress \fIid\fP
.B \-\-step
.IR k / n
.RI ( k
alone once the total is known; never more than it),
.B \-\-fraction
.IR f ,
.B \-\-note
.IR text .
.TP
.B task wait \fIid\fP \-\-for input|dependency|limit
Marks a task as blocked, with an optional
.B \-\-note
and the answers a person can pick
.RB ( \-\-option ,
repeated).
.TP
.B task status
The caller's run, and only it, as a compact tree for an agent: the run id, its percent,
then one line per task with its id, state, steps, and age or note. Finished groups are one
line.
.B \-\-titles
adds titles.
.TP
.B task guide
Short instructions an agent can follow: whether it is the orchestrator or working on a
tagged task, when to plan, how to delegate, and what each outcome means.
.TP
.B task adopt \fIrun\fP
Makes this harness session report into an earlier run, after a resume or
.B /clear
gave it a new session id. Commands and hooks then use that run.
.TP
.B task hook
Reads a Claude Code or Codex hook payload on standard input and records it: permission and
idle notifications as waiting for input, prompts as resumed, subagents starting and
stopping, the agent stopping and the session ending, plus the session's host app and
folder. Each call notes when the harness's hooks last ran. After a compaction, resume or
.BR /clear ,
SessionStart hands the run's tree back to the agent. Always exits 0. With
.B \-\-enforce
on Stop, it sends the agent back once when tasks it declared are still running with no
agent on them, unless the harness reports work still running in the background.
.TP
.B hooks install|uninstall|status
Adds, removes or shows the hooks that run
.B headroom task hook
in
.I ~/.claude/settings.json
(or
.IR $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/settings.json ;
.BR \-\-project :
.IR ./.claude/settings.json ;
.BR \-\-settings
.IR path ),
and a permission rule,
.BR "Bash(headroom task:*)" ,
so reports don't prompt.
.B \-\-codex
uses Codex's
.I ~/.codex/hooks.json
(or
.IR $CODEX_HOME/hooks.json )
instead; Codex runs new hooks once you trust them in its
.B /hooks
view.
.B \-\-instructions
also adds or removes a few lines, between
.B <!\-\- headroom:begin \-\->
and
.BR "<!\-\- headroom:end \-\->" ,
asking the agent to run
.B headroom task guide
in
.I ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md
or
.IR ~/.codex/AGENTS.md .
Install keeps every other setting, hook and key order, writes only when something changes,
follows symlinks, and writes a
.I .headroom-backup
beside the file first; uninstall removes only Headroom's entries. Status shows when a hook
last ran.
.TP
.B mcp
Optional. A stdio Model Context Protocol server with the tools
.B get_headroom
(the allowance),
.B get_tasks
(task runs),
.B task_status
and
.B task_guide
(as above), and
.B plan_tasks
and
.B update_task
(the same operations, checks and answers as
.BR "headroom task" ).
Without a
.B run
argument a tool uses the session the server started in, resolved on every call.
Add it with:
.B claude mcp add \-\-scope user headroom \-\- ~/.local/bin/headroom mcp
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; for
.BR wait ,
capacity is back.
.TP
.B 2
.B wait
timed out.
.TP
.B 1
A file could not be written or read
.RB ( task ,
.BR hooks ).
.TP
.B 3
No snapshot exists yet (open Headroom.app).
.TP
.B 64
Usage error, including a task id that isn't in a run with a plan (the message names the
closest ones).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disables colors.
.TP
.B HEADROOM_HOME
Reads the snapshot and task files from this directory instead.
.TP
.BR HEADROOM_RUN ", " CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID ", " CODEX_THREAD_ID ", " T3CODE_THREAD_ID
The run id for task commands and hooks, in this order, when neither
.B \-\-run
nor a
.IB run :
prefix on the task id gives one. A hook's payload
.B session_id
comes after
.B HEADROOM_RUN
and before the others. A session that ran
.B task adopt
uses the run it adopted.
.TP
.BR CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR ", " CODEX_HOME
Where
.B hooks
finds Claude Code's and Codex's settings and instructions files.
.TP
.BR __CFBundleIdentifier ", " TERM_PROGRAM
The app a session runs in, recorded on the run (never synced).
.SH FILES
.I ~/Library/Application Support/Headroom/snapshot.json
.br
.I ~/Library/Application Support/Headroom/tasks.json
(runs folded by the app)
.br
.I ~/Library/Application Support/Headroom/tasks/journal/<run>.jsonl
(declared events, appended by this command)
.br
.I ~/Library/Application Support/Headroom/tasks/observed/<run>.jsonl
(events the app reads from harness transcripts)
.br
.I ~/Library/Application Support/Headroom/tasks/aliases/<session>
(the run a session adopted)
.br
.I ~/Library/Application Support/Headroom/tasks/hook-heartbeat.json
(when each harness's hooks last ran)
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
headroom
headroom statusline \-\-format tmux
headroom wait \-\-provider claude && claude \-p "continue the refactor"
headroom task plan \-\-file plan.json
headroom task progress api/tests \-\-step 2/4
headroom task done api/schema api/tests \-\-evidence "12 tests pass"
headroom task status
headroom hooks install \-\-enforce \-\-instructions
headroom tasks \-\-watch
headroom wait \-\-run "$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID" \-\-until attention
.fi
